  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nIt's been another pleasant early October day out there today \nwith temperatures early this afternoon in the lower and middle \n70s and only a few passing high level clouds overhead. \nConditions will remain quiet through the night as temperatures \ndrop into the upper 40s and lower 50s by early Friday.\n\nA prominent center of high pressure is parked over the Ozark \nPlateau this afternoon and we find a region of warm advection \ndeveloping over the north-central Plains as the high helps to \npump mild southerly flow into the region. This warm advective \nwing will focus into Iowa this evening. Deep isentropic ascent \nand a corridor of frontogenetic forcing will foster an area of \nprecip to our west tonight. This feature will sharpen as it gets\nshunted east across northern IL tomorrow morning into \nafternoon. Scattered showers are expected in our area for much \nof the daytime beginning as early as predawn near and west of \nI-39. Highest coverage through the day is generally favored near\nand north of the Kankakee River valley along a theta-e gradient\nthat looks to develop on the northern periphery of the warm \nadvective push, although rain will be possible anywhere around \nthe area. Have boosted PoPs in the forecast and expanded to now \nbring at least slight chances to the whole area through the day.\n\nAs the low levels continue to warm, mid level lapse rates will \nsteepen through the day such that some embedded elevated \nthunderstorms will also be possible, especially after mid-\nmorning. There remains some uncertainty in how much instability \ncan build atop the area. The main pool of instability will \nremain west of the Mississippi while models generally top out \nbelow 1,000 Joules of elevated CAPE across northern IL by the \nafternoon. The general lack of instability should hinder a \nstrong storm threat, but 30 to 40 kt of effective shear may \nallow some cores to organize. Coverage should mostly exit the \nCWA by the end of the afternoon. Modeled QPF is largely on the \norder of one to two tenths or less, but many camps, especially \nthe convective-allowing camps, suggest more noteworthy amounts \nare possible on an isolated basis with the potential for pockets\nof heavier rain and thunderstorms.\n\nThere's quite a bit of spread in temperature guidance tomorrow \nwith uncertainty in the exact northward extent of the warm \nadvection. Most of the area is favored in the 70s but could be \nstuck in the 60s near and north of I-80. Saturday should be much\nwarmer with highs progged in the lower 80s area-wide and mostly\nsunny skies into the afternoon. Cloud cover looks to build \nlater in the day as the remnants of Hurricane Isaias work their \nway up into the region.\n\nThe core of the storm will stay to our south and southeast as \nit tracks across the Tennessee and Ohio valleys, but it's still \nunclear how far northwest layer moisture will stretch that can \nsupport precip Saturday night into Sunday. These remnants will \nbe clashing with a notably dry airmass over the Midwest and the \nbetter synoptic forcing will also focus to our south and east. \nIn the forecast, maintained slight chance PoPs as far northwest \nas the I-55 corridor during the overnight and morning periods \nwith low-end chances east of I-57 and into northwest IN.\n\nAdditional opportunities for rain are in the forecast next week\nincluding along a cold front Monday-night into Tuesday and with\nanother large synoptic system favored to traverse the region in\nthe Wednesday to Thursday window. Prior to that cold front, \nMonday will be another unseasonably warm day with highs progged \nin the upper 70s and lower 80s. More seasonable temperatures are\nthen favored into late week.\n\nDoom",
